  "step_3": "The decision reason requires evaluating outcomes with probability > 1/2 (i.e., > 0.5) to be 'probable' — but neither lottery has any outcome with such a probability. Therefore, both lotteries have zero probable outcomes, and the reason would be indifferent. The individual, however, does not refer to any such threshold or classification of outcomes. Instead, they base their choice on the relative size of the losses and the perceived insignificance of the 0.1% probability difference. Their reasoning is based on loss aversion and perception of small differences, not on the rule of probable outcomes. Since the individual does not consider or apply the rule about probable outcomes (which are absent in both lotteries), the decision reason was not used. There is no evidence that they evaluated outcomes based on the 1/2 threshold or computed an average of probable outcomes. The justification is entirely different from the decision reason's rule.",
