{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates lotteries based on the concept of 'probable outcomes'. An outcome is considered probable if its probability is higher than 1 divided by the number of outcomes. In this case, Lottery A has two outcomes: 1000 Euros with 100% probability. Lottery B has two outcomes: 3000 Euros with 50% probability and -3000 Euros with 50% probability. Thus, the number of outcomes is 2. The threshold for a probable outcome is 1/2 = 0.5. Therefore, an outcome is probable if its probability > 0.5. In Lottery A, the 1000 Euros outcome has 100% probability (>0.5), so it is probable. In Lottery B, both outcomes have 50% probability, which is not greater than 0.5, so neither outcome is considered probable. Thus, Lottery A has one probable outcome (1000 Euros), while Lottery B has zero probable outcomes. The reason prefers the lottery with a higher average of probable outcomes — in this case, A has one, B has zero — so A is preferred. The reason is applicable and well-defined for this decision problem.",
  "step_2": "The individual first looks at the left side (Lottery A), noting that it has a 100% probability of 1000 Euros. The individual observes that on the right side (Lottery B), there are extreme gains and losses (3000 and -3000 Euros) with only 50% probability. The individual justifies the choice by stating that the extreme differences in the outcomes of Lottery B are too large relative to their low probability. The individual chooses the safer option (Lottery A) because the extreme variations in B are not justified by their probabilities. There is no mention of calculating probable outcomes based on the threshold of 1/2 or comparing the number of probable outcomes. The justification focuses on the perceived imbalance between the magnitude of outcomes and their probabilities, rather than on the formal definition of probable outcomes or their average count.",
  "step_3": "The information relevant to the decision reason — identifying probable outcomes as those with probability > 0.5 and counting them — is not reflected in the verbal report. The individual does not refer to the threshold of 1/2 or evaluate which outcomes meet that criterion. Instead, the individual relies on an intuitive assessment of extreme outcomes being disproportionate to their probabilities. While the individual correctly identifies that Lottery B has more extreme outcomes and chooses the safer option, the reasoning is based on a different principle (risk aversion due to extreme variability) rather than the formal rule of probable outcomes. Therefore, the individual did not apply the specified decision reason. The justification does not match the rule of the reason. Thus, the decision reason was not used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
